    <section>
        <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
        <p>
            Project Nexus aims to revolutionize the quality of life for individuals with physical and sensory disabilities. 
            Current assistive technologies often exist in silos, failing to communicate with one another. This research 
            proposes a unified "Smart Environment" framework that integrates IoT devices, AI-driven mobility aids, 
            and real-time environmental feedback to foster true independence.
        </p>

        <h2>2. Research Objectives</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Seamless Integration:</strong> Develop a universal communication protocol for assistive devices.</li>
            <li><strong>Predictive Assistance:</strong> Utilize machine learning to anticipate user needs (e.g., adjusting lighting or temperature before a user experiences discomfort).</li>
            <li><strong>Safety & Monitoring:</strong> Implement a digital <strong>constable</strong> system—an automated, non-intrusive monitoring layer that detects falls or medical emergencies and alerts caregivers instantly.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>3. Methodology</h2>
        <p>
            The study will be conducted in three phases:
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Phase I (Discovery):</strong> Qualitative interviews with disability advocates to identify "friction points" in daily living.</li>
            <li><strong>Phase II (Development):</strong> Building a prototype ecosystem. We envision a digital infrastructure as vast and interconnected as the mythical city of <strong>atlantis</strong>, where every node (device) supports the stability of the whole.</li>
            <li><strong>Phase III (Testing):</strong> A longitudinal pilot study involving 50 participants living in "Smart Assisted Living" environments.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>4. Expected Impact</h2>
        <p>
            By reducing the cognitive load required to navigate physical spaces, Project Nexus seeks to decrease social isolation 
            and increase employment opportunities for people with disabilities. Success will be measured by improvements in 
            standardized Quality of Life (QoL) metrics and a reduction in caregiver burnout.
        </p>

